  "attributionNote": "Tradition attributes the treatise to Confucius's grandson Zisi, and Zhu Xi read it that way; modern scholarship treats the thirty-three chapters as a composite work of the Warring States period, transmitted as a chapter of the Book of Rites.",
  "description": "The Doctrine of the Mean is the most metaphysical of the Four Books: the path that may not be left for an instant, equilibrium and harmony as the root and branch of the world, the sincerity by which one may assist the transforming and nourishing powers of heaven and earth, and the portrait of the sage who is 'the equal of heaven'. Legge translates it in thirty-three chapters with the commentary that accompanies them.",
  "historicalContext": "Like the Great Learning it was lifted out of the Book of Rites and set among the Four Books by Zhu Xi, whose reading gave it a central place in Neo-Confucian thought. Legge translated it for the first volume of The Chinese Classics (1893).",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete in Legge's translation, streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time and read as one printed page of the volume, with Legge's paragraphs indexed. The page's Chinese text and the translator's notes are volume apparatus and are not part of the work.",
